Hello and welcome back to
another video with ItsDrDan.

00:00:04.019 --> 00:00:08.469
And what are we gonna be learning
about today is matter specifically

00:00:08.469 --> 00:00:12.250
with how do we look at matter
when it comes to chemistry?

00:00:12.719 --> 00:00:15.690
Uh, we are gonna be going through
this video from an introductory

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:20.880
standpoint, just so you can get the
fundamentals of how we look at things.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:24.240
So in this course, the big
thing that we like to understand

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:25.800
is we go from the ground up.

00:00:26.345 --> 00:00:31.595
And assuming that matter is, exists
as particles, so the smallest type

00:00:31.605 --> 00:00:33.415
of pieces you can possibly imagine.

00:00:33.894 --> 00:00:38.594
So, things like atoms, or even, what
even makes up atoms are smaller.

00:00:39.225 --> 00:00:42.015
So, when we look at particles,
what exactly are we talking about?

00:00:42.024 --> 00:00:42.935
How small?

00:00:43.355 --> 00:00:47.119
Well, here I have a photo, as you
can see, of someone spraying a mist.

00:00:47.470 --> 00:00:49.510
And we're talking on a
really, really small scale.

00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:52.780
So if you think of this mist, that's
all here, and you think of the finest

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:56.040
little droplets that are in here,
we're kind of going to zoom on that

00:00:56.110 --> 00:00:57.450
and we're looking at particles.

00:00:57.775 --> 00:01:00.555
That are about that size and even smaller.

00:01:00.565 --> 00:01:05.315
So imagine that small, but another
million or so times smaller than that.

00:01:05.694 --> 00:01:11.605
So as small as you can possibly go down to
the atomic scale, whenever we are looking

00:01:11.605 --> 00:01:15.114
at these things, we want to think of all
these little specs and the way that we're

00:01:15.114 --> 00:01:19.074
looking at these particles is that they're
all spaced out and that generally we just.

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:23.830
portray how to, let's look at how we
exactly we portray all these little

00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:25.330
different particles moving forward.

00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:25.800
When

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:38.350
we're looking at particles, the
whole idea is that they are made up

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:40.450
of atoms from your periodic table.

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:46.109
Now, particle, I'm using that as
a very generic term just to refer

00:01:46.119 --> 00:01:47.480
to something that's really small.

00:01:48.110 --> 00:01:52.235
So in a lot of times when it comes
to courses, We like to show these as

00:01:52.285 --> 00:01:56.865
very, very tiny atoms, and they're
usually represented by little dots.

00:01:57.295 --> 00:02:01.614
So, whenever we have those little dots,
this is a way for us to really be able

00:02:01.614 --> 00:02:07.174
to imagine and see all the different
atoms that are within a gas, or maybe

00:02:07.174 --> 00:02:09.664
a solid, or any type of material.

00:02:10.435 --> 00:02:14.055
Now, when it comes to molecules, well,
usually you'll see these little dots

00:02:14.055 --> 00:02:19.285
and they're all stuck together, they're
glued together, meaning they are bonded.

00:02:19.295 --> 00:02:23.795
So we'll use this word chemical
bonding very often from now on out.

00:02:24.085 --> 00:02:26.434
So you'll see the dots are connected.

00:02:26.844 --> 00:02:30.485
So what happens if these things are
connected and they're close together?

00:02:30.665 --> 00:02:32.525
How can we look at these pictures?

00:02:32.954 --> 00:02:36.685
So first off is looking
at the phases of matter.

00:02:37.155 --> 00:02:40.185
So matter comes in many different
forms, so there's solids,

00:02:40.225 --> 00:02:42.865
liquids, gases, and also plasmas.

00:02:43.294 --> 00:02:46.605
But we're going to be focusing on those
first three because they're what makes

00:02:46.605 --> 00:02:49.354
the most things that you will come across.

00:02:50.315 --> 00:02:53.965
So solids, usually you are going
to be exposed to these around you.

00:02:54.275 --> 00:02:59.785
So these are typically in a very
organized pattern, meaning that you will

00:02:59.805 --> 00:03:02.845
commonly see them as a repeating pattern.

00:03:03.275 --> 00:03:07.094
And we like to say that these
are crystalline as a result.

00:03:07.685 --> 00:03:11.364
So crystalline, as you can see,
like we have this pattern, right?

00:03:11.574 --> 00:03:15.989
Then we have 1, 2, 3.

00:03:16.309 --> 00:03:21.749
Repeating, so many of these solids that
we have all around us, whether even a lot

00:03:21.749 --> 00:03:25.450
of things that you have in front of you
have a specific crystalline structure.

00:03:25.870 --> 00:03:29.479
If you look at all different geodes and
rocks, you actually see that many of

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:31.539
them have all kinds of unique structures.

00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:32.300
in them.

00:03:32.660 --> 00:03:35.980
In fact, if you look at sodium chloride
or table salt that you have at home,

00:03:36.290 --> 00:03:41.079
it actually exists in a cubic shape on
the atomic level if you were to zoom in

00:03:41.079 --> 00:03:43.170
on it with a very powerful microscope.

00:03:43.950 --> 00:03:46.480
Now, do all solids exist this way?

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:49.560
No, if you kind of think of
things that are like rubber or

00:03:49.569 --> 00:03:51.380
they have an elastic character.

00:03:51.940 --> 00:03:55.480
These things are generally called
amorphous because they are more

00:03:55.820 --> 00:03:59.220
disorderly and they just kind of
flop on top of each other, but

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:01.300
they're still tightly bound together.

00:04:01.579 --> 00:04:06.829
They have a very high density typically
because of how close these are together.

00:04:07.469 --> 00:04:14.510
So solids have a very definite, uh, volume
and they have a definite shape behind it.

00:04:14.530 --> 00:04:15.550
And that's the important thing.

00:04:15.550 --> 00:04:19.920
It's a definite volume
and shape versus liquids.

00:04:20.130 --> 00:04:22.610
Liquids do not have a definite shape.

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:25.600
But they do have a definite volume.

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:31.469
Meaning that the atoms are still very,
very close together and they more kind

00:04:31.469 --> 00:04:34.320
of slide and slither past each other.

00:04:34.599 --> 00:04:38.809
Solids are all nice and close and
hunkered together, very nice and packed

00:04:38.809 --> 00:04:41.249
where liquids are more go with the flow.

00:04:41.670 --> 00:04:44.060
They're more relaxed as a result.

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:49.810
So these can, um, they will take the
volume of whatever container they're in.

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:53.230
So you think of a liquid, if I have
a cup of water, right, it's going

00:04:53.230 --> 00:04:54.750
to take the shape of that cup.

00:04:55.205 --> 00:04:58.255
If I were to dump the water
onto the countertop, it's now

00:04:58.265 --> 00:04:59.525
the shape of your counter.

00:04:59.784 --> 00:05:02.515
Or if it falls onto the floor,
it's now the shape of the floor.

00:05:02.795 --> 00:05:05.315
It just takes whatever
thing holds it together.

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:08.219
Um, but you can measure the volume, right?

00:05:08.219 --> 00:05:12.890
You could take all that water, put it in
a grad cylinder, measure it, and be able

00:05:12.890 --> 00:05:15.380
to see that it has a very exact volume.

00:05:16.839 --> 00:05:21.260
Now when it comes to gases, gases,
they do not have a definite shape

00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:25.319
or a definite volume, meaning
that you can compress them.

00:05:25.789 --> 00:05:27.459
Now why is that exactly?

00:05:27.459 --> 00:05:31.919
And that's because atoms are like,
or gas atoms are like little billiard

00:05:31.929 --> 00:05:34.579
balls that are free moving and.

00:05:35.010 --> 00:05:39.650
Um, they exist very, very far apart,
meaning that between them, there's

00:05:39.650 --> 00:05:42.510
a ton of empty space between them.

00:05:42.939 --> 00:05:46.510
They move extremely fast,
generally has to do with the

00:05:46.550 --> 00:05:48.360
temperature that is behind them.

00:05:48.719 --> 00:05:52.730
So if they have a lot of kinetic energy
within them, they will move very quickly.

00:05:52.999 --> 00:05:57.030
And if they are, have a lower temperature,
they'll move very, very slowly.

00:05:57.785 --> 00:06:01.865
So typically you can try to
describe these things as very

00:06:01.865 --> 00:06:03.775
fast moving most of the time.

00:06:04.125 --> 00:06:07.125
So I like to think of gas particles
kind of like bouncing all over the

00:06:07.125 --> 00:06:11.215
place, like a five year old hyped up
on sugar at a birthday party being

00:06:11.215 --> 00:06:14.355
that they are going so incredibly fast.

00:06:15.165 --> 00:06:17.495
So we have those three phases.

00:06:17.765 --> 00:06:20.045
How can we try to classify?

00:06:20.970 --> 00:06:22.900
All these, all the
things around us, right?

00:06:22.910 --> 00:06:25.710
You can look around, maybe your room or
wherever it is that you're watching this

00:06:25.710 --> 00:06:29.989
video, and you can see many different
forms of matter around you, whether it's

00:06:29.989 --> 00:06:35.010
your laptop, your phone, maybe your desk,
where maybe your chair, your clothing,

00:06:35.020 --> 00:06:36.940
you, all these things are different.

00:06:37.479 --> 00:06:39.539
So how can we classify matter?

00:06:39.639 --> 00:06:42.220
Well, the way that we do it
in the scientific communities,

00:06:42.220 --> 00:06:43.850
we do it by composition.

00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:46.050
What is it made up of?

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:49.130
There's two ways we can classify this.

00:06:49.570 --> 00:06:54.179
We can either classify them as a
pure substance, meaning that it's

00:06:54.299 --> 00:06:57.119
only made up of one type of particle.

00:06:57.740 --> 00:07:02.919
One type meaning one atom
or one specific compound.

00:07:03.150 --> 00:07:04.369
It could be one or the other.

00:07:05.319 --> 00:07:10.650
Mixtures are whenever we have
essentially more than one type.

00:07:10.650 --> 00:07:15.390
So if you take, uh, let's say
if you had air, for example.

00:07:15.430 --> 00:07:19.310
Air has oxygen, nitrogen,
many other gases.

00:07:19.690 --> 00:07:20.900
That would be a mixture.

00:07:21.340 --> 00:07:28.110
Now, if you had, um, let's say only,
if you take like your salt shaker and

00:07:28.110 --> 00:07:32.629
there's only sodium chloride inside
of it, that would be a pure substance.

00:07:33.089 --> 00:07:35.809
So it can exist in a
couple different ways.

00:07:35.839 --> 00:07:40.360
Pure substances can be both
elements and compounds, so it can

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:45.320
be both, and then mixtures can be
separated into two different forms

00:07:45.320 --> 00:07:47.250
as well, which are homogeneous.

00:07:47.655 --> 00:07:50.025
and heterogeneous mixtures.

00:07:50.795 --> 00:07:55.375
Let's take a look at both so that way
we can understand exactly what they mean

00:07:55.385 --> 00:07:57.425
and how we can understand what they are.

00:07:58.375 --> 00:08:02.454
So the first one, which was the
elements and the pure substances.

00:08:03.025 --> 00:08:08.655
So, these are when you have just one type
of atom, meaning that it could be, if you

00:08:08.655 --> 00:08:13.404
take your p roc table, it could be any one
of those different atoms that are there.

00:08:14.165 --> 00:08:18.644
Now, all atoms, they don't always
necessarily exist by themselves,

00:08:18.645 --> 00:08:20.165
like this little top picture here.

00:08:20.665 --> 00:08:25.595
Sometimes they might be bound to
themselves in different arrangements.

00:08:26.085 --> 00:08:28.415
However, they are still just one atom.

00:08:28.955 --> 00:08:33.024
So, an example like this is
something like O2, oxygen gas, right?

00:08:33.025 --> 00:08:37.385
You're breathing in O2 right now,
but it's still one element, but

00:08:37.385 --> 00:08:38.804
they just happen to be connected.

00:08:39.284 --> 00:08:41.025
So it's a little
different than a compound.

00:08:41.985 --> 00:08:45.875
Um, now other ways that you can have these
different elements all connected together

00:08:45.875 --> 00:08:47.885
is when you have things called allotropes.

00:08:47.944 --> 00:08:52.055
We really don't discuss them a lot in this
class, but it's just kind of nice to know.

00:08:52.395 --> 00:08:54.295
Um, so if you think of carbon, right?

00:08:54.295 --> 00:08:58.304
So if you burn something and that soot
that's left behind, that's carbon.

00:08:58.795 --> 00:09:01.515
Now, if you put that under a
lot of pressure, you could get

00:09:01.515 --> 00:09:03.545
coal or you can get graphite.

00:09:03.775 --> 00:09:06.985
Or if you put it under enough pressure
and time, you can get diamonds.

00:09:07.484 --> 00:09:10.155
All of those are all carbon, right?

00:09:10.215 --> 00:09:11.675
And we look at a diamond versus coal.

00:09:11.685 --> 00:09:15.074
They look nothing alike, but it's
all based on pressure and time.

00:09:16.025 --> 00:09:19.714
So these are all different
versions of a pure element.

00:09:20.334 --> 00:09:24.154
Now, the most important pure elements
for you to remember without just having

00:09:24.154 --> 00:09:29.604
your periodic table is realizing that
whenever an element bonds to itself,

00:09:29.884 --> 00:09:35.964
and there's only two of those atoms
bound together, so like O2, H2, N2,

00:09:36.275 --> 00:09:38.525
these are referred to as diatomics.

00:09:38.975 --> 00:09:44.395
So diatomic gases, there's seven of
them, I refer to them as the diatomic

00:09:44.405 --> 00:09:50.455
seven, uh, you need to memorize them
for any chemistry course, or even any

00:09:50.455 --> 00:09:55.295
future higher level chem courses, even
some, uh, if you're in respiratory

00:09:55.295 --> 00:10:00.154
therapy or any type of biology courses
as well, they come up quite often.

00:10:00.625 --> 00:10:04.439
So these seven types of gases, in
order to remember them, There's

00:10:04.449 --> 00:10:10.910
hydrogen, nitrogen gas, fluorine,
oxygen, iodine, chlorine, and bromine.

00:10:11.560 --> 00:10:16.709
Now, trying to remember all seven of
these diatomic gases can be a little

00:10:16.709 --> 00:10:20.829
tricky, so I came up, I have a mnemonic
that I love to use I found online.

00:10:21.565 --> 00:10:25.415
And it's have no fear of ice cold beer.

00:10:25.915 --> 00:10:30.815
So if you remember that, right, the first
letter of each of these words represents

00:10:30.825 --> 00:10:34.835
one of these elements that are over here
and I color coded them to help match.

00:10:35.195 --> 00:10:39.174
So if you remember, have no fear
of ice cold beer, you will never

00:10:39.174 --> 00:10:40.644
forget your seven diatomics.

00:10:41.155 --> 00:10:46.305
So H for have, so that'd be hydrogen
and N would be nitrogen and so on.

00:10:47.005 --> 00:10:48.365
This will be very helpful for you.

00:10:48.375 --> 00:10:50.935
You need to memorize
them for future courses.

00:10:51.830 --> 00:10:54.099
Now, these are your elements.

00:10:54.829 --> 00:10:59.820
Compounds are, just to talk about them,
right, it's whenever you have two or more

00:10:59.840 --> 00:11:05.910
elements that are bound together, meaning
they exist in a very fixed proportion.

00:11:06.659 --> 00:11:08.849
One example of this is water, right?

00:11:08.850 --> 00:11:12.509
You drink water every single day,
hopefully, if you're taking care of

00:11:12.510 --> 00:11:18.860
yourself, but it always exists as two
hydrogen atoms to every one oxygen atom.

00:11:19.185 --> 00:11:20.454
It's in a ratio.

00:11:20.965 --> 00:11:22.885
Now, this is a pure substance.

00:11:22.885 --> 00:11:27.095
If you have a clean glass of water and it
went through a filter, you can drink it.

00:11:27.125 --> 00:11:28.635
And it is only water.

00:11:29.215 --> 00:11:34.775
Now, if you decide to, if you have
salt as well, so sodium chloride.

00:11:35.225 --> 00:11:36.885
in that solution as well.

00:11:37.105 --> 00:11:40.875
Well, now all of a sudden you have
more of a mixture as a result, right?

00:11:40.875 --> 00:11:43.664
It could be soluble, it could
be homogeneous, heterogeneous,

00:11:44.035 --> 00:11:45.325
you can classify that.

00:11:46.025 --> 00:11:50.214
But sodium chloride, NaCl, notice
that when you have this crystal

00:11:50.224 --> 00:11:55.785
pattern, it's this repeating lattice,
so crystalline structure, but this

00:11:55.795 --> 00:11:59.324
is a pure solid of only Na and Cl.

00:11:59.825 --> 00:12:02.435
at ions that are connected together.

00:12:03.275 --> 00:12:05.464
So you have that specific portion.

00:12:05.834 --> 00:12:10.865
Um, when you are looking at these
now, um, pounds, they can also do all

00:12:10.865 --> 00:12:14.664
kinds of cool chemistry that you can
break them apart and they decompose

00:12:15.035 --> 00:12:17.595
into completely different pieces.

00:12:17.904 --> 00:12:19.075
So you can see that.

00:12:19.215 --> 00:12:21.015
So that's what we're going
to look at right now.

00:12:21.175 --> 00:12:21.455
Right?

00:12:21.455 --> 00:12:24.515
So what we're seeing here is
someone's putting sugar in this

00:12:24.535 --> 00:12:28.715
container and they're dumping
sulfuric acid onto the sugar.

00:12:28.975 --> 00:12:32.425
And immediately you notice
the properties of this, these

00:12:32.425 --> 00:12:34.325
materials are immediately changing.

00:12:34.925 --> 00:12:37.935
The sugar, right, is normally
a white crystalline solid.

00:12:38.375 --> 00:12:41.875
The sulfuric acid was this clear solution.

00:12:42.275 --> 00:12:44.694
Now all of a sudden you can see
that the chemical properties

00:12:44.695 --> 00:12:45.584
are immediately changing.

00:12:45.585 --> 00:12:48.224
They're very reactive together, right?

00:12:48.224 --> 00:12:49.795
So they right change to black.

00:12:49.795 --> 00:12:52.695
Now if I unpause it, you're going
to start seeing that it's already,

00:12:52.715 --> 00:12:53.755
they're starting to bubble.

00:12:53.825 --> 00:12:58.045
If you look very, very closely right here
and notice how there's bubbles forming.

00:12:58.260 --> 00:13:01.530
And if you mix it and give it a little
bit of an agitation, you can see

00:13:01.530 --> 00:13:04.930
the sugars dissolving and more and
more bubbles are starting to form.

00:13:05.190 --> 00:13:08.070
It's going to start turning
darker and darker and a lot of

00:13:08.100 --> 00:13:10.000
heat is going to be released.

00:13:10.289 --> 00:13:14.609
So meaning that the temperature is
going to be changing and therefore

00:13:14.609 --> 00:13:19.459
you're going to see this, um, it's very
thermodynamic process is occurring.

00:13:19.910 --> 00:13:23.050
So if I unpause it again and this
happening in real time as well,

00:13:23.360 --> 00:13:27.500
you can see all of a sudden it,
the volume is changing of this.

00:13:27.860 --> 00:13:28.550
material.

00:13:28.670 --> 00:13:30.440
That's because you're
creating something new.

00:13:30.490 --> 00:13:31.770
You're creating a new chemical.

00:13:32.050 --> 00:13:35.420
So the compound can, pure
substance can dissolve and

00:13:35.420 --> 00:13:36.969
turn into something brand new.

00:13:37.599 --> 00:13:43.929
So what this pure compound or sugar is
decomposing into is you can see it's

00:13:43.930 --> 00:13:46.949
turning into pure elemental carbon.

00:13:47.355 --> 00:13:50.985
So this is all carbon that's coming
out of this, so it's like a big carbon

00:13:50.985 --> 00:13:55.035
snake that's coming off, and you can
see all of this gas that's, that's,

00:13:55.055 --> 00:14:00.905
uh, completely evaporating off, um,
because of how hot this thing can be.

00:14:01.204 --> 00:14:02.534
This is chemistry in action.

00:14:02.685 --> 00:14:05.174
It's really one of my favorites,
but this thing's a big mess, so I

00:14:05.175 --> 00:14:06.254
don't know if we do it in the lab.

00:14:06.395 --> 00:14:09.275
The next part of our series
is looking at mixtures.

00:14:09.655 --> 00:14:13.145
So there, there's homogeneous,
which means homo meaning same,

00:14:13.334 --> 00:14:15.344
and then heterogeneous mixtures.

00:14:15.895 --> 00:14:20.375
So what this is all about is that when you
have multiple pure compounds and they're

00:14:20.375 --> 00:14:23.365
all mixed together This makes a mixture.

00:14:23.875 --> 00:14:29.465
So homogeneous is when you can't
physically separate them So or so you

00:14:29.465 --> 00:14:33.915
can't tell just by looking at it that
there is a difference between them.

00:14:33.915 --> 00:14:35.155
They are uniform.

00:14:35.525 --> 00:14:41.645
So an example of this would be something
like, um, if you have, uh, like air

00:14:41.675 --> 00:14:46.905
or many other different types of
mixtures, like all these solutions of

00:14:46.915 --> 00:14:51.115
nickel that I have up here, notice how
they're blue, they're purple, they're,

00:14:51.455 --> 00:14:54.525
um, like a yellowy green, a teal.

00:14:54.900 --> 00:14:59.380
So, all these are mixtures of
nickel with water, and they all

